To override std::hash for a custom type in C++, you can follow these steps:

1. Include the header file for the hash function: #include <functional>
2. Define a struct or class that overloads the function operator(). This will be the custom hash function for your type.
3. Inside the function operator(), use the std::hash function to hash the data members of your custom type. You can use the std::hash function on individual data members or on a combination of data members to calculate the overall hash value.
4. Specify the custom hash function as the template parameter for the std::unordered\_map, std::unordered\_set, or other container that you are using.

For example, consider a custom type called "Person" with data members "name" and "age":
```
#include <functional>

struct Person {
  std::string name;
  int age;

  bool operator==(const Person& other) const {
    return name == other.name && age == other.age;
  }
};

struct PersonHash {
  std::size_t operator()(const Person& p) const {
    std::size_t h1 = std::hash<std::string>()(p.name);
    std::size_t h2 = std::hash<int>()(p.age);
    return h1 ^ (h2 << 1);  // XOR the hash values to combine them
  }
};

int main() {
  std::unordered_set<Person, PersonHash> people;
  // ...
}
```
Note that the custom hash function should also be used as the template parameter for the std::equal\_to function when defining the key\_equal member function for the container.

