Yes, the Uppy.io SDK API can retrieve the hash value of local drag and drop files. Uppy.io provides several plugins that can be used to manipulate and analyze files, including the "Hash" plugin, which calculates and returns the hash of a file.

To use the Hash plugin with Uppy.io, you need to install it as a dependency in your project, and then add it to your Uppy instance. Here is an example code snippet that shows how to use the Hash plugin to get the hash value of a local file:
```javascript
import Uppy from '@uppy/core';
import Hash from '@uppy/hash';

const uppy = Uppy({
  id: 'myUppy',
  autoProceed: true,
  restrictions: {
    maxFileSize: 1000000,
    maxNumberOfFiles: 3,
    minNumberOfFiles: 1,
    allowedFileTypes: ['image/*', 'video/*']
  }
});

uppy.use(Hash);

uppy.on('file-added', (file) => {
  uppy.getFile(file.id).then((file) => {
    const hash = file.hash;
    console.log(`Hash for file ${file.name}: ${hash}`);
  });
});
```
In this example, the "Hash" plugin is added to the Uppy instance using the `use()` method. Then, when a file is added to the upload queue (triggered by the "file-added" event), the hash value is retrieved using the `getFile()` method, and then printed to the console.

Note that the hash value is calculated asynchronously, so you need to use the `then()` method to wait for the calculation to complete before accessing the hash value.

