In computer programming, both queue and stack are data structures used to store and organize data. The main difference between them is the way data is accessed and removed.

A queue follows the "First-In-First-Out" (FIFO) principle, which means that the first item that is added to the queue is the first one to be removed. In contrast, a stack follows the "Last-In-First-Out" (LIFO) principle, which means that the last item added to the stack is the first one to be removed.

Here's an example of implementing a queue and stack in C++ using the STL (Standard Template Library):
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <stack>

using namespace std;

int main() {
  // Queue example
  queue<int> q;

  q.push(10);
  q.push(20);
  q.push(30);

  cout << "Queue: ";
  while (!q.empty()) {
    cout << q.front() << " ";
    q.pop();
  }
  cout << endl;

  // Stack example
  stack<int> s;

  s.push(10);
  s.push(20);
  s.push(30);

  cout << "Stack: ";
  while (!s.empty()) {
    cout << s.top() << " ";
    s.pop();
  }
  cout << endl;

  return 0;
}
```
In this example, we first declare a `queue<int>` and a `stack<int>` to store integers. We then add three elements to both data structures using the `push()` function.

To display the contents of the queue, we use a `while` loop that continues until the queue is empty. Inside the loop, we print the front element of the queue using the `front()` function and remove it using the `pop()` function.

To display the contents of the stack, we use a similar `while` loop, but instead of `front()`, we use the `top()` function to retrieve the top element of the stack, and then remove it using `pop()`.

The output of the program should be:
```makefile
Queue: 10 20 30 
Stack: 30 20 10
```
which demonstrates the difference in ordering between a queue and a stack.

