Here is the refactored version using Array.reduce:
```
const uniqueData = this.data.reduce((acc, item) => {
  const id = item.company.id;
  if (!acc.has(id)) {
    acc.set(id, item);
    return acc;
  }
  const otherItem = acc.get(id);
  acc.set(id, {
    ...item,
    startDate: this.$dayjs.min(
      this.$dayjs(otherItem.startDate),
      this.$dayjs(item.startDate),
    ),
    endDate: this.$dayjs.max(
      this.$dayjs(otherItem.endDate),
      this.$dayjs(item.endDate),
    ),
  });
  return acc;
}, new Map());

return [...uniqueData.values()];
```
The Array.reduce method allows you to reduce an array to a single value by applying a function to each element in the array. In this case, we are using reduce to build up a Map of unique items, where the key is the `id` of the item's `company` and the value is the item itself. We use the Map's `set` method to add new items to the map or update existing ones. Finally, we return an array of the values from the Map using the `values` method and the spread operator (`...`).

