Here's an example of how you can use web workers to download CSV data in an Ember Octane component:
```typescript
import Component from '@glimmer/component';
import { action } from '@ember/object';

export default class CsvDownloaderComponent extends Component {
  @action
  downloadCsv() {
    const csvData = 'Name,Email\nJohn,john@example.com\nJane,jane@example.com\n';
    const blob = new Blob([csvData], { type: 'text/csv' });

    const worker = new Worker('/workers/csv-downloader.js');
    worker.postMessage(blob);

    worker.onmessage = (event) => {
      const downloadLink = document.createElement('a');
      downloadLink.href = URL.createObjectURL(event.data);
      downloadLink.download = 'data.csv';
      downloadLink.click();
    };
  }
}
```
This component defines an `@action` method called `downloadCsv()` that is triggered when the user clicks a button or some other UI element. The method creates a `Blob` object containing the CSV data, and then creates a new web worker using the `Worker` constructor. The `Blob` object is sent to the worker using the `postMessage()` method.

The web worker code is located in a separate file called `csv-downloader.js`. Here's an example implementation of that file:
```javascript
self.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
  const blob = event.data;

  const reader = new FileReader();
  reader.readAsDataURL(blob);

  reader.onload = (event) => {
    const url = event.target.result;
    const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
    xhr.open('GET', url);
    xhr.responseType = 'blob';
    xhr.onload = () => {
      self.postMessage(xhr.response);
    };
    xhr.send();
  };
});
```
The web worker listens for messages from the main thread using the `addEventListener()` method. When it receives a message, it reads the `Blob` data using a `FileReader`, and then sends a GET request to the same URL using `XMLHttpRequest`. The response is a `Blob` object containing the CSV data, which is then sent back to the main thread using `postMessage()`.

When the main thread receives the response from the web worker, it creates a new download link and sets its `href` to the URL of the `Blob` object. The `download` attribute is set to "data.csv" to give the file a default name. Finally, the link is clicked using the `click()` method to initiate the download.

Note that web workers are subject to same-origin policy restrictions, so both the main thread and the web worker must be served from the same origin in order for this example to work.

