To connect to the URL, retrieve the XML, and parse it into an `IEnumerable` model in C#, you can use the `HttpClient` and `XDocument` classes.

Here's some sample code to get you started:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Xml.Linq;

public class ExchangeRate
{
    public string Currency { get; set; }
    public decimal Value { get; set; }
}

public static class ExchangeRateService
{
    private const string Url = "https://www.bsi.si/_data/tecajnice/dtecbs.xml";

    public static IEnumerable<ExchangeRate> GetExchangeRates()
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            var response = client.GetAsync(Url).Result;
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            var xml = XDocument.Load(response.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync().Result);
            var ns = xml.Root.Name.Namespace;

            return xml.Root
                .Element(ns + "tecajnice")
                .Elements(ns + "tecajnica")
                .Where(e => (string)e.Attribute("drzava") == "EMU")
                .Elements(ns + "tecaj")
                .Select(e => new ExchangeRate
                {
                    Currency = (string)e.Attribute("oznaka"),
                    Value = (decimal)e.Attribute("sifra")
                });
        }
    }
}
```
This code defines an `ExchangeRate` class with `Currency` and `Value` properties. It also defines a `ExchangeRateService` class with a `GetExchangeRates` method that retrieves the XML from the URL, parses it using `XDocument`, and returns an `IEnumerable<ExchangeRate>` containing the exchange rates for the EMU countries.

Note that in this example, I assumed that you only want to retrieve the exchange rates for the EMU countries. If you want to retrieve all the exchange rates, you can remove the `.Where(e => (string)e.Attribute("drzava") == "EMU")` line from the LINQ query.

