To implement a trace header in ASP.NET Core, you can use middleware. The middleware component would inspect incoming requests and add the trace header to the response before it is sent back to the client. Here is an example of how you can implement this in ASP.NET Core:
```csharp
public class TraceHeaderMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    public TraceHeaderMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
    {
        _next = next;
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        var traceId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();
        context.Response.OnStarting(() =>
        {
            context.Response.Headers["X-Trace-Id"] = traceId;
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        });
        await _next(context);
    }
}

public static class TraceHeaderMiddlewareExtensions
{
    public static IApplicationBuilder UseTraceHeader(this IApplicationBuilder builder)
    {
        return builder.UseMiddleware<TraceHeaderMiddleware>();
    }
}
```
In your `Startup.cs` file, you can add the middleware to the pipeline using the `UseTraceHeader` extension method:
```java
public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    ...
    app.UseTraceHeader();
    ...
}
```
With this implementation, every incoming request will have a unique trace header `X-Trace-Id` added to the response.

